```js
const serializeValue = (value, delimiter = ',') => {
  if (value == null) return '';
  const s = String(value);
  const needsQuotes = s.includes(delimiter) || s.includes('"') || s.includes('\n');
  return needsQuotes ? `"${s.replaceAll('"', '""')}"` : s;
};
const serializeRow = (row, delimiter = ',') =>
  row.map(value => serializeValue(value, delimiter)).join(delimiter);

serializeRow(['a', null]); // 'a,'
serializeRow(['a,b', 'c']); // '"a,b",c'
serializeRow(['a"b', 'c']); // '"a""b",c'
serializeRow(['a\nb', 'c']); // '"a\nb",c'
```

Standard CSV (RFC 4180) keeps the real newline inside the quotes; do not replace it with a `\n` text escape, or spreadsheet apps will show the backslash. A classic bug is passing a template string like `` `value` `` instead of the variable, which serializes the word "value" for every cell.

