Use a generator: walk the iterable with `for...of`, push each value into the current `chunk`, and `yield` it whenever it reaches `size`, starting a new one; after the loop, yield the leftover partial chunk. `slice()` doesn't work here because a general iterable has no length or indexes.

```js
function* chunkify(itr, size) {
  let chunk = [];
  for (const v of itr) {
    chunk.push(v);
    if (chunk.length === size) {
      yield chunk;
      chunk = [];
    }
  }
  if (chunk.length) yield chunk; // don't lose the last partial chunk
}

const ids = new Set([1, 2, 1, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5]); // {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
[...chunkify(ids, 2)]; // [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]]
```

The generator is lazy: each chunk is built only when the consumer asks for the next one, e.g. `for (const batch of chunkify(rows, 100)) await insert(batch);`.

