Throttling makes `fn` run at most once every `wait` milliseconds, no matter how often the event fires. This version runs immediately if enough time has passed, and otherwise schedules one trailing call with the latest arguments.

```js
const throttle = (fn, wait) => {
  let lastTime = 0;
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    const remaining = wait - (Date.now() - lastTime);
    clearTimeout(timer);
    if (remaining <= 0) {
      lastTime = Date.now();
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else {
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        lastTime = Date.now();
        fn.apply(this, args);
      }, remaining);
    }
  };
};

window.addEventListener('resize', throttle(() => {
  console.log(window.innerWidth);
}, 250)); // logs at most every 250ms, plus once at the end
```

Throttle vs debounce: throttle gives steady updates while events continue (scroll position, resize). Debounce waits until events stop (search-as-you-type). `fn.apply(this, args)` keeps the caller's `this` and arguments.

