back:
Scope is the region of code where a name is visible. Global names are visible everywhere; names declared inside a function are visible only in that function (and functions nested in it); `let`/`const` inside a `{ }` block are visible only in that block, while `var` ignores blocks and belongs to the enclosing function.

```js
const appName = 'Recall';         // global: visible everywhere

function save() {
  const url = '/api/cards';       // function scope: only inside save()
  if (true) {
    let saved = true;             // block scope: only inside this if
    var attempts = 1;             // var: visible in all of save()
  }
  console.log(attempts);          // 1
  console.log(saved);             // ReferenceError: saved is not defined
}
console.log(url);                 // ReferenceError: url is not defined
```

Inner scopes can read outer ones, never the reverse. A `var` from a block keeps its assigned value in the rest of the function; it is `undefined` only if read before the assignment runs.

Use `const` by default, `let` when you need to reassign the variable, and avoid `var`. `let` and `const` are block-scoped and throw if used before their declaration; `var` is function-scoped, leaks out of blocks, can be redeclared silently, and reads as `undefined` before its line.

```js
const items = [];      // never reassigned (you can still push to it)
let total = 0;         // reassigned in the loop
for (const item of cart) {
  items.push(item.id);
  total += item.price;
}
```

`const` prevents reassignment, not mutation: `items.push()` is fine, `items = []` throws a `TypeError`. You will still see `var` in older code, so know how it behaves, but don't write new `var`s.

A clear message for humans (what went wrong, with the bad value, and ideally how to fix it) plus a specific error type for code (a built-in like `TypeError`/`RangeError`, or your own `Error` subclass with its own `name`). Callers can then branch on the type while logs stay readable.

```js
// Built-in types for common cases
throw new TypeError(`Expected a string for email, got ${typeof email}`);
throw new RangeError(`Page must be 1-${lastPage}, got ${page}`);

// Your own type for domain errors
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message, options) {
    super(message, options);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
  }
}

try {
  saveUser(form);
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) showFieldError(err.message);
  else throw err; // not ours to handle: let it propagate
}
```

Setting `this.name` matters: it is what shows up in logs and stack traces (`ValidationError: Email is required`) instead of a generic `Error`.

