Wrap it: `throw new Error('Failed to load user 42', { cause: err })`. The new message says what your code was trying to do, and `cause` (ES2022) keeps the original error and its stack for debugging. Handle the error instead only when it is expected and you have a real response, such as retrying or showing a message.

```js
async function loadUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`); // fetch doesn't reject on 404/500
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to load user ${id}`, { cause: err });
  }
}
```

Being "vocal" doesn't mean crashing everywhere; it means every failure ends up somewhere visible: rethrown with context, logged with `console.error`, or shown to the user. Note that `fetch` only rejects on network failure, so always check `res.ok` yourself.

