Add `n * 3_600_000` ms (one hour = `1000 * 60 * 60`) to the timestamp: `d.setTime(d.getTime() + n * 3_600_000)`. That moves the instant by exactly N real hours. On a DST day the local clock jumps, so +24 hours can land at 13:00 instead of 12:00; for "same time tomorrow" use `setDate(getDate() + 1)`.

```js
const addHoursToDate = (date, n) => {
  const d = new Date(date);
  d.setTime(d.getTime() + n * 3_600_000);
  return d;
};

const start = new Date(2020, 9, 19, 12, 0); // Oct 19 2020, 12:00 local
addHoursToDate(start, 10).getHours();  // 22
addHoursToDate(start, -10).getHours(); // 2

// US time zones, DST starts Mar 10 2024:
addHoursToDate(new Date(2024, 2, 9, 12), 24).getHours(); // 13, not 12
```

Rule of thumb: durations (timeouts, token expiry) use milliseconds; calendar steps (tomorrow, next month) use the `setDate`/`setMonth` family.

