Move the date to the **Thursday of its week**; that Thursday's year is the week-year, and its day-of-year / 7 (rounded up) is the week number. Do the math in UTC so DST can't skew it.

```js
const weekOfYear = date => {
  const d = new Date(Date.UTC(date.getFullYear(), date.getMonth(), date.getDate()));
  const day = d.getUTCDay() || 7;          // Mon = 1 … Sun = 7
  d.setUTCDate(d.getUTCDate() + 4 - day);  // Thursday of this week
  const yearStart = Date.UTC(d.getUTCFullYear(), 0, 1);
  return Math.ceil(((d - yearStart) / 86_400_000 + 1) / 7);
};

weekOfYear(new Date(2021, 5, 18)); // 24
weekOfYear(new Date(2021, 0, 1));  // 53 (belongs to 2020's last week)
weekOfYear(new Date(2024, 11, 30)); // 1 (belongs to 2025's week 1)
```

Why Thursday: ISO says week 1 is the week containing the first Thursday, so a week always belongs to the year its Thursday is in. US-style week numbers (Sunday start) differ; use one scheme consistently.

