Because characters like `&`, `=`, `?`, `#` and spaces have meaning in a URL. `encodeURIComponent()` escapes everything except `A–Z a–z 0–9 - _ . ! ~ * ' ( )`, so the value stays one value. Use it for a single piece of a URL (a parameter value or path segment), never for a whole URL.

```js
const q = 'rock & roll';

`/search?q=${q}`;                     // '/search?q=rock & roll' → q is 'rock '
`/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`; // '/search?q=rock%20%26%20roll'

// Easiest: let URL/URLSearchParams encode for you
const url = new URL('/search', 'https://shop.example');
url.searchParams.set('q', q);
url.href; // 'https://shop.example/search?q=rock+%26+roll'
```

`encodeURIComponent('https://a.com/x')` gives `'https%3A%2F%2Fa.com%2Fx'`: fine as a parameter value, but no longer a usable link.

It leaves the characters that give a URL its structure (`; , / ? : @ & = + $ #`) plus letters, digits and `- _ . ! ~ * ' ( )`. That makes it suitable for a **complete URL** that just needs spaces or non-ASCII characters escaped, but a parameter value containing `&` or `#` passes through unescaped and breaks the URL.

```js
encodeURI('https://my-website.com/my-page?query="a%b"&user=1');
// 'https://my-website.com/my-page?query=%22a%25b%22&user=1'

encodeURI('my-page#with,speci@l&/"characters"?');
