Look the element up once and reuse the reference: every `querySelector` call searches the document again, and repeating it adds noise and small costs. The bigger cost is usually **layout thrashing**: mixing DOM reads (like `offsetHeight`) with writes forces the browser to recalculate layout repeatedly.

```js
const saveButton = document.querySelector('#save');
saveButton.disabled = true;
saveButton.textContent = 'Saving…';
saveButton.classList.add('is-loading');

// Thrashing: read, write, read, write... in a loop
items.forEach(el => { el.style.height = `${el.offsetHeight + 10}px`; });
// Better: do all reads first, then all writes
const heights = items.map(el => el.offsetHeight);
items.forEach((el, i) => { el.style.height = `${heights[i] + 10}px`; });
```

A stored reference to a removed element keeps it in memory only while the variable is reachable. Local variables are freed normally; only long-lived references (globals, caches, closures) need clearing.

