It lets you declare *rules* in a JSON `<script type="speculationrules">` block: which URLs or link patterns may be prefetched or prerendered, and how eagerly. The browser then speculates based on user behaviour (hover, pointer down), instead of you hand-picking URLs.

```html
<script type="speculationrules">
{
  "prefetch": [{
    "where": { "href_matches": "/products/*" },
    "eagerness": "moderate"
  }]
}
</script>
```

Support is Chromium-first (Chrome, Edge, Opera); other engines have lagged, so check caniuse. Browsers without support ignore the script type, so it's a safe progressive enhancement. Prerendering is limited to same-origin pages by default, and speculations are listed in DevTools' Application panel under Speculative loads.

Use a `"prerender"` rule instead of `"prefetch"`. The browser fetches the page *and* renders it, running its JavaScript, in a hidden tab, so navigation feels instant.

```html
<script type="speculationrules">
{ "prerender": [{ "urls": ["/dashboard", "/orders"] }] }
</script>
```

Because scripts run before the user ever sees the page, page-view analytics or "mark as read" calls could fire for pages never visited. Check `document.prerendering` and defer such work until the `prerenderingchange` event.

```js
if (document.prerendering) {
  document.addEventListener('prerenderingchange', trackPageView, { once: true });
} else {
  trackPageView();
}
```

Use `"where"` with an `"and"` of conditions: `href_matches` (URL Pattern syntax, `*` wildcards) to include links, and `"not"` clauses to exclude URL patterns or CSS selectors.

```html
<script type="speculationrules">
{
  "prefetch": [{
    "where": { "and": [
      { "href_matches": "/*" },
      { "not": { "href_matches": "/logout" } },
      { "not": { "href_matches": "/*\\?*(^|&)add-to-cart=*" } },
      { "not": { "selector_matches": "[rel~=nofollow]" } }
    ] },
    "eagerness": "moderate"
