Regex syntax is dense: nearly every character can be an operator, a plain pattern has no names or comments, and pieces can't be shared, so a long pattern is hard to read, risky to change, and copied instead of reused. A builder library replaces symbols with named, composable functions (`zeroOrMore(digit)` instead of `\d*`).

```js
// Hard to scan:
/^(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})$/

// Built-in help without a library: named groups
const m = '2024-05-01'.match(/^(?<year>\d{4})-(?<month>\d{2})-(?<day>\d{2})$/);
m.groups.month; // '05'
```

Before reaching for a library, plain JavaScript already helps: named groups, building patterns from well-named constants, and unit tests that document what should and shouldn't match.

Written as one literal, nobody can tell which part handles which range, so changing it means re-deriving the whole thing. Built from named helpers, each branch gets a comment and can be tested and changed on its own.

```js
const digit = String.raw`\d`;
const range = (a, b) => `[${a}-${b}]`;
const or = (...p) => `(?:${p.join('|')})`;
const concat = (...p) => p.join('');
const optional = (p) => `(?:${p})?`;

const octet = or(
  concat(optional(range(0, 1)), digit, optional(digit)), // 0-199
  concat(2, range(0, 4), digit),                         // 200-249
  concat(25, range(0, 5)),                               // 250-255
);

const isOctet = new RegExp(`^${octet}$`);
isOctet.test('255'); // true
isOctet.test('256'); // false
new RegExp(octet).test('256'); // true: no anchors, it matches "25"
```

The last line is a classic validation bug: without `^...$` a regex only needs to match *part* of the input. Always anchor patterns that validate a whole value.

