Race it against a timer promise that **rejects** after the limit: `Promise.race([promise, timeout(3000)])` settles with whichever finishes first, so if the timer wins you get a rejection.

```js
const timeout = ms =>
  new Promise((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`Timed out after ${ms} ms`)), ms)
  );

const withTimeout = (promise, ms) => Promise.race([promise, timeout(ms)]);

const slow = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('data'), 5000));
withTimeout(slow, 3000)
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.log(err.message)); // 'Timed out after 3000 ms'
```

Pitfall: `Promise.race` does not **cancel** the losing promise; the slow work keeps running. For `fetch`, prefer the built-in signal, which actually aborts the request:

```js
const res = await fetch('/api/users', { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(3000) });
// rejects with a 'TimeoutError' DOMException if it takes longer
```

One solution:

```js
Promise.race([promise, timeout(ms)]);
```

`Promise.race` settles the same way as the first promise in the array to settle. If `timeout(ms)` rejects first, the result rejects with `'Timed out'`. If `promise` settles first, you get its value or error.

Remember that the race does not stop the slow promise; it just stops waiting for it. For `fetch`, use `{ signal: AbortSignal.timeout(ms) }` to really abort the request.

```js
const timeout = ms =>
  new Promise((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timed out')), ms)
  );

const withTimeout = (promise, ms) =>
  Promise.race([promise, timeout(ms)]);

const slow = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('data'), 500));
withTimeout(slow, 100).catch(err => console.log(err.message)); // 'Timed out'
```

Because the timer keeps running after the real promise wins: its callback still fires later, and a pending timer keeps a Node process (a script, a test run) alive until it goes off. Save the timer id and call `clearTimeout(id)` in `.finally()` on the race.

```js
const withTimeout = (promise, ms) => {
  let id;
  const timer = new Promise((_, reject) => {
    id = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timed out')), ms);
  });
  return Promise.race([promise, timer]).finally(() => clearTimeout(id));
};

withTimeout(Promise.resolve('fast'), 60_000).then(v => console.log(v));
// logs 'fast' and the process exits at once instead of waiting 60 s
```

If you create many timeouts (say one per request), you can put this in a small class that keeps each id in an array and clears them all in one `clear()` call. The idea is the same: whoever starts a timer is responsible for clearing it.

JavaScript has no built-in, so check each kind of value explicitly: **falsy values** (`null`, `undefined`, `''`, `0`, `false`, `NaN`), **whitespace-only strings**, **empty arrays and plain objects**, **empty `Map`/`Set`**, and **invalid `Date`s**. Check the type first, then the "emptiness" rule for that type.

```js
const isBlank = value => {
  if (!value) return true; // null, undefined, '', 0, false, NaN
  if (typeof value === 'string') return value.trim() === '';
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.length === 0;
  if (value instanceof Map || value instanceof Set) return value.size === 0;
  if (value instanceof Date) return Number.isNaN(value.getTime());
  if (Object.getPrototypeOf(value) === Object.prototype)
    return Object.keys(value).length === 0;
  return false;
};

isBlank('  \n'); // true
isBlank({}); // true
isBlank(new Date('hello')); // true
isBlank(new Date()); // false
isBlank(new Map([['a', 1]])); // false
```

Pitfall: a generic "no own keys means empty" rule is wrong: a valid `Date`, a non-empty `Map` and a function all have zero own keys. Also decide deliberately whether `0` and `false` are blank: for a "quantity" or "is admin" field they are real answers.

