Wrap the state in a **Proxy whose `set` trap stores the value and then dispatches an event** on an `EventTarget`. Listeners subscribe with `addEventListener(propertyName, ...)` and receive the new value in `event.detail`. This is the Observer pattern: the subject notifies its observers when it changes.

```js
const createStore = initial => {
  const events = new EventTarget();
  const state = new Proxy(initial, {
    set(target, key, value) {
      target[key] = value;
      events.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(key, { detail: value }));
      return true;
    },
  });
  const on = (key, fn) => events.addEventListener(key, e => fn(e.detail));
  return { state, on };
};

const store = createStore({ name: '' });
store.on('name', value => console.log(`Name changed to ${value}`));
store.state.name = 'Alice'; // logs 'Name changed to Alice'
```

`EventTarget` and `CustomEvent` exist in both browsers and Node, so this runs anywhere. Only top-level assignments are observed: `state.user.name = 'x'` or `state.items.push(x)` changes a nested object without going through the trap. Libraries such as Vue's reactivity system wrap nested objects too.

