`instanceof` checks whether a constructor's `prototype` is in an **object's** prototype chain. A primitive like `'abc'` is not an object, so the check is always `false`. Only a wrapper made with `new String('abc')` passes. A class can override the check with a **static `[Symbol.hasInstance]` method**: `x instanceof C` then calls `C[Symbol.hasInstance](x)` and uses its result.

```js
'abc' instanceof String; // false
new String('abc') instanceof String; // true

class PrimitiveString {
  static [Symbol.hasInstance](x) {
    return typeof x === 'string';
  }
}

'abc' instanceof PrimitiveString; // true
42 instanceof PrimitiveString; // false
```

This is a neat demonstration of how `instanceof` works, but in application code just write `typeof x === 'string'`. Overriding `instanceof` surprises readers.

