Listen for the `animationiteration` event on the animated element: it fires each time a looping (`infinite`) CSS animation starts a new cycle, so the handler can set the next word while the text is faded out.

```css
.rotating-word { animation: fade-cycle 2s ease-in-out infinite; }

@keyframes fade-cycle {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 0; }
  15%, 85% { opacity: 1; }
}
```

```js
const words = ['Java', 'Python', 'C#', 'JavaScript'];
const el = document.querySelector('.rotating-word');
let i = 0;
el.textContent = words[0];
el.addEventListener('animationiteration', () => {
  i = (i + 1) % words.length;   // wrap back to 0
  el.textContent = words[i];    // textContent, not innerHTML
});
```

Swap text at the invisible point of the cycle (opacity 0) so the change is not seen. Accessibility: constantly changing text is noisy for screen readers. Give the real sentence to assistive tech (e.g. a visually hidden "Java, Python, C#…" list), put `aria-hidden="true"` on the rotating word, and stop the loop under `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`.

With `border-box`, the `width` and `height` you set include padding and border, so a `width: 200px` box stays 200px wide however much padding you add. The default, `content-box`, adds padding and border on top of `width`.

```css
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

.card {
  width: 200px;
  padding: 16px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  /* content-box: 200 + 32 + 2 = 234px wide
     border-box:  exactly 200px wide */
}
```

This matters for layouts like `width: 50%` columns with padding: under `content-box` two of them add up to more than 100% and wrap. The pseudo-elements are included because `*` does not match `::before`/`::after`. Margin is never part of either box size. A variant sets `border-box` on `html` and `box-sizing: inherit` on everything else, which makes it easy for a third-party widget to switch its subtree back to `content-box`.

Add `::before`/`::after` content to the `<img>`. A loaded image is a replaced element and never renders pseudo-elements. When loading fails, some browsers render the `<img>` like a normal inline box, and then the pseudo-elements appear.

```css
img { display: block; width: 100%; text-align: center; }

