By default the browser does not know a custom property's type; it treats the value as an arbitrary token string, so it can't interpolate between 0 and 100 and just jumps. Register it with `@property` and a `syntax` such as `"<number>"`. The browser can then tween it like any numeric property.

```css
@property --progress {
  syntax: "<number>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: 0;
}

@keyframes fill {
  from { --progress: 0; }
  to   { --progress: 100; }
}

.circular-progress {
  animation: fill 5s linear forwards;   /* forwards = keep the end value */
}
```

This gives a timed progress bar (e.g. "reward in 5s") with no JavaScript. For a countdown that empties, add `reverse` to the animation and keep `forwards`. With `reverse`, `forwards` holds the `from` keyframe (0) after the animation ends. `animation-fill-mode: backwards` only affects the delay before the animation starts. `@property` works in all modern browsers.

Custom properties chain the math: radius → circumference → dash length. Then `stroke-dasharray: var(--dash) calc(var(--circumference) - var(--dash))` draws exactly that much of the ring. Changing or animating `--progress` is all you need to update the bar.

```css
.circular-progress {
  --size: 250px;
  --stroke-width: 20px;
  --radius: calc((var(--size) - var(--stroke-width)) / 2);
  --circumference: calc(2 * pi * var(--radius));
  --dash: calc(var(--progress) * var(--circumference) / 100);
}

.circular-progress circle.fg {
  transform: rotate(-90deg);
  transform-origin: center;
  stroke-dasharray: var(--dash) calc(var(--circumference) - var(--dash));
  stroke-linecap: round;
}
```

`--progress` must be registered with `@property` (as `<number>`) if you want to animate it. From JS, `el.style.setProperty('--progress', 42)` updates the bar. Also keep `aria-valuenow` in sync so screen readers get the value.

Floats are taken out of normal flow, so the parent doesn't grow to contain them. The classic clearfix adds an empty `::after` block that clears both floats and forces the parent to extend below them. The modern fix is `display: flow-root`. It makes the parent a new block formatting context, and a block formatting context always contains its floats.

```css
/* Legacy clearfix */
.clearfix::after {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  clear: both;
}

/* Modern */
.container { display: flow-root; }
```

Today, use flexbox or grid for layout and keep `float` for its original job: wrapping text around an image. You will still see clearfix in older codebases and CSS frameworks.

