Aim for roughly 150–300ms. Small changes (hover colors, button presses) feel best around 100–200ms; larger movements (menus, modals, panels) around 250–400ms. Anything much slower makes the UI feel sluggish. The research usually cited is the Doherty Threshold (1982): productivity rises when the system responds within about 400ms, so neither side waits on the other.

```css
:root {
  --dur-fast: 150ms;
  --dur-base: 250ms;
}
.dropdown { transition: opacity var(--dur-base) ease-out, translate var(--dur-base) ease-out; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * { transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; }
}
```

Strictly, the Doherty Threshold is about system response time, not animation length, but it's a useful ceiling: a transition should never make users wait. Exits can be a bit faster than entrances. Keep durations in shared variables so they stay consistent.

