back:
Give every action the same shape, `{ type, payload }`; move each case's logic into a small named handler (or let Redux Toolkit's `createSlice` generate the reducer); and define action types once as constants or generated names. The reducer then reads like a table of contents.

```js
const handlers = {
  'user/setName': (state, name) => ({ ...state, name }),
  'user/addTag': (state, tag) => ({ ...state, tags: [...state.tags, tag] }),
};

function userReducer(state, { type, payload }) {
  const handle = handlers[type];
  return handle ? handle(state, payload) : state;
}

dispatch({ type: 'user/addTag', payload: 'react' });
```

Why the shape matters: with a fixed `payload` key, nobody has to remember whether this action used `action.name`, `action.value`, or `action.data`. Small handlers are easy to test on their own. In Redux apps, `createSlice` is the standard way to get all three.

By default **all of them, recursively**, whether or not their props changed. Rendering just means calling the components again; React then compares the results with the previous render and touches the DOM only where the output actually changed.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Sidebar />        {/* re-renders on every click, though it has no props */}
    </>
  );
}
```

Ways to skip work: wrap a child in `React.memo` (skip when props are equal), or let the React Compiler memoize automatically. Elements passed in as `children` from higher up are not re-created when this component's state changes, so they don't re-render. Usually extra renders are cheap; optimize only when profiling shows a slow component.

Each `useSelector` subscribes to the store by itself. After every dispatch it runs its selector and re-renders its component **only if the selected result changed** (compared with `===`). Context, by contrast, re-renders every consumer whenever the provider's `value` changes. The cost: every mounted selector runs on every dispatch, so selectors must be cheap and must not return new objects each time.

```jsx
// Re-renders on EVERY dispatch: filter() returns a new array each time
const done = useSelector(state => state.todos.filter(t => t.done));

// Better: select plain values, or memoize the derived array
const doneCount = useSelector(state => state.todos.filter(t => t.done).length);
const selectDone = createSelector([s => s.todos], todos => todos.filter(t => t.done));
const done2 = useSelector(selectDone);
```

`mapStateToProps` with `connect` works the same way, but hooks are the recommended API for new code.

React compares the provider's `value` with `Object.is`. The object literal is a **new object on every render**, so React treats the context as changed and re-renders every component that reads it, even if `user` is the same. Memoize the value so it only changes when its contents do.

```jsx
const UserContext = createContext(null);

function UserProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return <UserContext value={value}>{children}</UserContext>;
}

function Avatar() {
  const { user } = use(UserContext);   // or useContext(UserContext)
  return <img src={user?.avatarUrl} alt="" />;
}
```

React 19 lets you render `<UserContext value={...}>` directly (`.Provider` still works) and read it with `use()`. Context itself only passes a value down; the state still lives in `useState`/`useReducer`.

They only help when something **compares the reference**: a child wrapped in `React.memo`, or a dependency array of another hook (`useEffect`, `useMemo`). Passed to a normal child, a memoized callback changes nothing, because that child re-renders with its parent anyway.

```jsx
const Row = memo(function Row({ item, onDelete }) { /* ... */ });

function List({ items }) {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');
  // Stable reference, so memo(Row) can skip rows when only `filter` changes
  const handleDelete = useCallback(id => deleteItem(id), []);
  return items.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} onDelete={handleDelete} />);
}
```

Also use `useMemo` for genuinely expensive calculations (e.g. sorting thousands of rows). Elsewhere they add code and memory for no gain. If your project uses the React Compiler, it inserts this memoization automatically, so manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` is rarely needed.

Move the state and the provider into their own component that renders `{children}`. The children elements are created by the component above, so when the provider's state changes they are not re-rendered; React only updates the components that read the context. Alternatively, wrap the component right under the provider in `React.memo`.

```jsx
function ThemeProvider({ children }) {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ theme, setTheme }), [theme]);
  return <ThemeContext value={value}>{children}</ThemeContext>;
}

function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeProvider>
      <Layout />   {/* not re-rendered when theme changes... */}
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
}

function ThemeToggle() {
  const { theme, setTheme } = use(ThemeContext); // ...but this one is
  return <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>{theme}</button>;
}
```

Context updates reach consumers even through memoized parents that skip rendering.

Because a React component should be a **pure function of its props, state, and context**: the same inputs always produce the same output, so re-running it can't produce anything new. `React.memo`, `useMemo`, and the React Compiler all rely on that. Your render code must not read changing outside values or mutate anything.

```jsx
// Impure: output depends on something React doesn't track
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <p>Hi {name}, it is {new Date().toLocaleTimeString()}</p>;
}

// Pure: the time comes in as state/props, so React knows when it changes
function Greeting({ name, now }) {
  return <p>Hi {name}, it is {now.toLocaleTimeString()}</p>;
}
```

If a memoized component is impure, skipping it shows stale UI. StrictMode calls components twice in development to help you catch impure rendering.

Rendering is React **calling your components** to get a description of the UI: a tree of plain JavaScript objects (React elements) produced from JSX. React compares that new tree with the tree from the **previous render**, not with the live DOM. This comparison is called reconciliation, and only the differences are then applied to the DOM.

```jsx
function Price({ amount }) {
  return <span className="price">${amount}</span>;
}
// Rendering <Price amount={5} /> produces roughly:
// { type: 'span', props: { className: 'price', children: ['$', 5] } }
```

So "a component rendered" does not mean "the page changed". If the new output equals the old one, React makes no DOM updates at all. JSX compiles to `jsx()` calls from `react/jsx-runtime` (older setups used `React.createElement`).

A `connect`ed component skips rendering when its parent re-renders with the same props. A component using `useSelector` re-renders **whenever its parent re-renders** (and also when its selected store data changes). If that matters, wrap the hook-based component in `React.memo`.

```jsx
const TodoItem = memo(function TodoItem({ id }) {
  const todo = useSelector(state => state.todos.byId[id]);
  return <li>{todo.title}</li>;
});
```

Hooks (`useSelector`, `useDispatch`) are the recommended API for new code; `connect` is the older higher-order-component API that you'll still meet in existing apps. Either way, passing new object or function references as props defeats the memoization.

**Render phase:** React calls your components and works out what changed. It doesn't touch the DOM, and it may pause, restart, or (in StrictMode) repeat this work, so rendering must be pure. **Commit phase:** React applies the changes to the DOM and attaches refs. `useLayoutEffect` runs right after the commit, **before** the browser paints; `useEffect` usually runs **after** the paint.

```jsx
function Tooltip() {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    // DOM exists, screen not painted yet: safe to measure and reposition
    console.log(ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().height);
  }, []);
  useEffect(() => {
    // after paint: subscriptions, logging, network sync
  }, []);
  return <div ref={ref}>Hi</div>;
}
```

Takeaways: rendering is not the same as updating the DOM, and a component can render without any visible change. Prefer `useEffect`; use `useLayoutEffect` only for measuring or adjusting layout before the user sees it.

Do everything in the click handler: measure the button, store the ripple's position with a unique id, and remove it in the ripple's `onAnimationEnd`. Use `e.currentTarget` (the element the handler is on, the button) because `e.target` can be the inner label `<span>`, which would give the wrong rectangle.

```jsx
function RippleButton({ children, onClick }) {
  const [ripple, setRipple] = useState(null);

  const handleClick = e => {
    const rect = e.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
    setRipple({ x: e.clientX - rect.left, y: e.clientY - rect.top, id: Date.now() });
    onClick?.(e);
  };

  return (
    <button type="button" className="ripple-button" onClick={handleClick}>
      {ripple && (
        <span
          key={ripple.id}                 // new key → remount → animation restarts
          className="ripple"
          aria-hidden="true"
          style={{ left: ripple.x, top: ripple.y }}
          onAnimationEnd={() => setRipple(null)}
        />
      )}
      <span className="content">{children}</span>
    </button>
  );
}
```

Effects that only react to your own state changes ("when coords change, set isRippling") add renders and bugs; here the old `setTimeout` was never cleared and could fire after unmount.

Animate `transform: scale()` to grow the circle and `opacity` to fade it, both in a `@keyframes` rule. `forwards` keeps the styles of the **last** keyframe after the animation ends; without it the circle would snap back to full opacity and small size for a moment.

```css
.ripple {
  animation: ripple-effect 0.9s ease forwards;
}

@keyframes ripple-effect {
  from { transform: scale(1);  opacity: 1; }
  to   { transform: scale(35); opacity: 0; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .ripple { animation: none; opacity: 0; }
}
```

`transform` and `opacity` are cheap to animate because they don't trigger layout, so the effect stays smooth on phones. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for users who turn animations off.

