It uses the same latest-callback ref, but `setTimeout` fires **once** after `delay`. The effect depends on `[delay]` and its cleanup calls `clearTimeout`, so unmounting cancels the pending call (no update on a removed component) and a new `delay` restarts the timer. `null` disables it.

```jsx
function useTimeout(callback, delay) {
  const savedCallback = useRef(callback);
  useEffect(() => {
    savedCallback.current = callback;
  }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (delay === null) return;
    const id = setTimeout(() => savedCallback.current(), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [delay]);
}

function Toast({ message, onDismiss }) {
  useTimeout(onDismiss, 5000);        // auto-close after 5 s
  return <div role="status">{message}</div>;
}
```

A new `callback` on each render does not restart the timer; only a new `delay` does. If you need to restart for the same delay (e.g. a new toast message), remount the component with a different `key`.

