On the server there's no `window`, and the first client render must produce the **same HTML** as the server for hydration to match. So the initial state is a placeholder, and the effect, which runs only in the browser after hydration, reads `innerWidth`/`innerHeight` and subscribes to `resize`.

```jsx
function useWindowSize() {
  const [size, setSize] = useState({ width: undefined, height: undefined });

  useEffect(() => {
    const update = () => setSize({ width: window.innerWidth, height: window.innerHeight });
    update();                                   // read the real size once
    window.addEventListener('resize', update);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', update);
  }, []);

  return size;
}
```

For layout (hide a sidebar, stack columns) prefer CSS media or container queries: no JavaScript, no re-renders, and no flash on first paint. Use the hook when logic needs the number, e.g. choosing how many chart points to render. In a client-only app you could initialize from `window` directly.

The server renders with `isBrowser = false`, but the browser's **first** render (hydration) computes `true`, so the two HTML trees differ and React reports a hydration error. Make the first client render match the server, then switch: either set a flag in an effect, or use `useSyncExternalStore` with a server snapshot of `false`.

```jsx
const subscribe = () => () => {};

function useIsClient() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,
    () => true,     // on the client after hydration
    () => false     // on the server and during hydration
  );
}

function LocalTime({ iso }) {
  const isClient = useIsClient();
  return <time dateTime={iso}>{isClient ? new Date(iso).toLocaleString() : iso}</time>;
}
```

Reading `window` in event handlers and effects is always safe, since they never run on the server. Only render output needs this care. In a client-only app (no server rendering) you don't need the hook at all.

Add a `beforeunload` listener **only while the form has unsaved changes**, and call `e.preventDefault()` in it. The browser then shows its own generic "Leave site?" dialog. Browsers ignore custom messages and block `alert`/`confirm` during unload, so you can't replace or customize that prompt.

```jsx
function useUnsavedChangesWarning(isDirty) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isDirty) return;
    const handler = e => {
      e.preventDefault();
      e.returnValue = true;    // legacy: Chrome/Edge < 119
    };
    window.addEventListener('beforeunload', handler);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', handler);
  }, [isDirty]);
}

// const isDirty = title !== savedTitle;  useUnsavedChangesWarning(isDirty);
```

Registering it only when dirty matters: a `beforeunload` listener can stop the browser's back/forward cache from working. It also doesn't catch in-app route changes in a single-page app; use your router's navigation blocker for those.

